# `--output` selects one of the outputs a target declares, out of all of them.
#
# The honest boundary is the whole reason this case is short. What `--output`
# *selects* is what is pinned here; a selector naming a platform this toolchain
# cannot build for is refused, and the refusal **names which of three things is
# missing** rather than saying one sentence for all of them.
#
# That is buri-lang/buri#25 and buri-lang/buri#26, and the goldens below are
# where the fix is pinned. The old wording was "the linux backend is not
# implemented", with a fix reading "this toolchain emits JavaScript; build with
# `--output=js`", and it was false twice over on the machine that printed it:
# the same rule's host-native output had just been built, so the toolchain
# plainly emits more than JavaScript, and the reason the cross one was refused
# was the *host* rather than any backend. `build/actions.rs::native_gap` is the
# one function all three refusal sites now ask.
#
# The **profile** half of the same question is not here and could not be: what
# `--release` answers for the host's own output depends on how this toolchain
# was built, so a golden would pin one leg of `cli/tests/README.md`'s bar and
# fail on the other. `compiler::backend`'s
# `a_release_refusal_names_the_profile_rather_than_the_platform` is where that
# half lives, with an arm for each leg.
#
# **Which platform that is depends on the machine**, so the case does not name
# one. `{{CROSS_PLATFORM}}` and `{{CROSS_ARCH}}` are filled in by the harness
# (cli/tests/harness/case.rs) with a platform/arch pair that is not the host's:
# linux/x86_64 on a mac, macos/x86_64 on Linux. The goldens hold the
# placeholder, so blessing on either machine records the same file.
#
# The two hosts do *not* answer a cross selector the same way, and that is the
# shape design/native/ARCHITECTURE.md §9 fixes: a Linux artifact is a
# self-contained static-PIE musl executable, so any host can build one; a macOS
# artifact links against Apple's libSystem stubs, which do not ship, so only a
# macOS host can. `build/link.rs::can_link` is that rule. So the cross selector
# is a **refusal** on a Linux host (its cross is macOS) and a **success** on a
# macOS host (its cross is Linux, cross-built and cached in `~/.buri`). Each run
# below names the host it is about with `host:`, and the harness runs it there.
# The refusal that stays honest on every machine is the one direction the rule
# forbids — Linux → macOS.
#
# What is deliberately *not* here is the positive half: a build for the host
# platform. That answer depends on how this toolchain was built — a
# `--no-default-features` binary has no backend and refuses its own host — so
# it is a claim about the toolchain rather than about `--output`, and it is
# pinned where the native suites can state it as one.
#
# Schema: the repository-case manifest, cli/tests/harness/case.rs. Textproto,
# but not a Buri file: only repo/ below is a Buri repository.

doc: "--output selects one declared output, names the declared ones when it selects none, and reaches the backend for a platform with none"

run {
  args: ["build", "//cmd/app", "--output=js"]
  exit: 0
  golden: "app_js.txt"
}

# //cmd/app declares one output and it is not this one. Selecting nothing at
# all used to build nothing and exit 0, which is a build system reporting
# success for work it did not do.
run {
  args: ["build", "//cmd/app", "--output={{CROSS_PLATFORM}}/{{CROSS_ARCH}}"]
  exit: 2
  golden: "no_such_output.txt"
}

# //cmd/native declares two. Selecting the one that exists builds it and
# nothing else.
run {
  args: ["build", "//cmd/native", "--output=js"]
  exit: 0
  golden: "native_js.txt"
}

# Selecting the other one. `{{CROSS_PLATFORM}}` is the platform that is not the
# host, and a Linux artifact builds on any host while a macOS one builds only on
# a macOS host (design/native/ARCHITECTURE.md §9) — so the two hosts answer this
# differently and each run names the host it is about.
#
# On a **Linux** host the cross platform is macOS, which no Linux host can
# build, so the backend refusal is what this selects. Exit 1: the selector was a
# real output, so the invocation was fine.
run {
  args: ["build", "//cmd/native", "--output={{CROSS_PLATFORM}}/{{CROSS_ARCH}}"]
  exit: 1
  golden: "native_cross.txt"
  host: LINUX
}

# The dash spelling is the directory under `.buri/out`, and selects the same
# entry as the slash spelling.
run {
  args: ["build", "//cmd/native", "--output={{CROSS_PLATFORM}}-{{CROSS_ARCH}}"]
  exit: 1
  golden: "native_cross.txt"
  host: LINUX
}

# On a **macOS** host the cross platform is Linux, which any host can build: the
# toolchain cross-builds the runtime archive and musl sysroot for the target,
# caches them in `~/.buri`, and links a static-PIE executable. So the same
# selector that refuses on Linux succeeds here, and this is the artifact.
run {
  args: ["build", "//cmd/native", "--output={{CROSS_PLATFORM}}/{{CROSS_ARCH}}"]
  exit: 0
  golden: "native_cross_built.txt"
  host: MACOS
}

# With no selector, every declared output is built. On Linux that is one
# artifact (js) and one refusal (the macOS native output).
run {
  args: ["build", "//cmd/native"]
  exit: 1
  golden: "native_all.txt"
  host: LINUX
}

# On macOS both declared outputs build: the JavaScript module and the
# cross-built Linux executable.
run {
  args: ["build", "//cmd/native"]
  exit: 0
  golden: "native_all_built.txt"
  host: MACOS
}

